WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:07.860
The Allocation Scandal. The National Institutes of Health has an annual budget of approximately forty-seven billion dollars.

2
00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:14.400
55 million people die annually. You would think those two facts would be related.

3
00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:22.440
They are not. A twenty twenty-three J.A.M.A. study analyzed two hundred forty-seven billion dollars in N.I.H.

4
00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:30.420
spending (two thousand ten to two thousand nineteen) and found: Eighty-four point nine percent went to basic research, totaling

5
00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:35.940
two hundred nine point nine billion dollars. Mice, molecules, mechanisms.

6
00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:40.800
"Experimental work without any particular application in view."

7
00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:48.940
They put the quiet part in the job description. Eleven point eight percent went to applied research, non-trial, totaling

8
00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:56.140
twenty-nine point three billion dollars. Research with "practical aims" that does not practically aim at testing treatments

9
00:00:56.140 --> 00:01:06.060
in humans. The clinical and translational science awards, or C.T.S.A., infrastructure program costs approximately one billion

10
00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:14.660
dollars per year in buildings, overhead, and salaries. Observational studies watch patients get sicker without testing treatments

11
00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:21.240
(the All of Us program spent two point one six billion dollars to observe disease happening.

12
00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:29.420
Heroic.). Other applied research includes drug characterization, health services research, and epidemiology.

13
00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:35.920
Three point three percent went to phased clinical trials, totaling eight point one billion dollars.

14
00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:40.200
Actually testing if drugs work in humans (Phases one through three).

15
00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:47.620
Phase one cost one point five billion dollars, or eighteen point five percent of the clinical trial budget.

16
00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:52.740
Phase two cost three point five billion dollars, or forty-three point two percent.

17
00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:58.140
Phase three cost two point six billion dollars, or thirty-two point one percent.

18
00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:06.480
The N.I.H. covers only three point seven to four point three percent of Phase three costs, then wanders off like a cat that

19
00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:13.860
lost interest. Pragmatic trials (thirty times cheaper) are severely underfunded despite proven efficiency.

20
00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:23.040
The N.I.H. spends ninety-six point seven percent of its budget on everything except the one thing that saves lives: testing

21
00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:30.700
treatments in humans. It's like spending ninety-seven percent of your grocery budget on cookbooks and three percent on food,

22
00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:34.510
then wondering why you're starving. This isn't just inefficiency.

23
00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:41.160
It's a body count. Why This Allocation Exists. This isn't bureaucratic accident.

24
00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:49.300
It's lobbied outcome. Drug companies love the N.I.H. Taxpayers absorb the risk of basic research; industry patents whatever

25
00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:55.000
works. It's like if your neighbor paid for your kitchen renovation, and then you charged them rent to eat there.

26
00:02:55.700 --> 00:03:00.160
But independent trials comparing drugs head-to-head? Those are terrifying.

27
00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:05.660
They might reveal that the eighty thousand dollar drug works about as well as the four dollar generic.

28
00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:10.740
PhRMA, the pharmaceutical lobby, has consistently opposed comparative effectiveness research.

29
00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:19.740
When Congress created the patient-centered outcomes research institute, or P.C.O.R.I., in two thousand ten, industry successfully

30
00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:24.680
lobbied to prohibit it from making coverage recommendations based on cost-effectiveness.

31
00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:30.780
Congress literally passed a law saying "don't fund research that might lower drug prices."

32
00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:37.920
They wrote it down. In legislation. On purpose. The revolving door helps.

33
00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:41.240
N.I.H. advisory committees include industry representatives.

34
00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:47.220
Former N.I.H. officials join pharmaceutical boards. Academic researchers depend on industry grants.

35
00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:55.020
Everyone's incentives align against cheap, fast trials testing whether expensive drugs outperform generics.

36
00:03:55.800 --> 00:04:03.660
It's not a conspiracy. It's just that everyone involved happens to benefit from the same terrible outcome, purely by coincidence,

37
00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:08.360
repeatedly, for decades. Basic research produces publications.

38
00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:16.730
Publications produce tenure. Tenure produces more grants. Nobody in this cycle needs anyone to get better.

39
00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:22.440
They need papers. The three point three percent allocation isn't a mystery.

40
00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:28.420
It's a business model. The Efficiency Gap: A Tale of Two Trials.

41
00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:33.680
The difference between efficient and inefficient trial design isn't theoretical.

42
00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:38.560
Humanity accidentally ran a controlled experiment on itself during COVID.

43
00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:43.560
Two countries. Same disease. One spent like it was trying to cure death.

44
00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:49.680
The other actually did. RECOVER Initiative (N.I.H. Approach).

45
00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:58.580
The budget is one point six six five billion dollars (one point one five billion dollars plus five hundred fifteen million

46
00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:02.780
dollars in twenty twenty-four). The timeline is four years and counting.

47
00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:08.490
Approximately thirty thousand patients are enrolled. Zero trials have been completed.

48
00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:12.860
The cost per patient is fifty-five thousand five hundred dollars.

49
00:05:13.820 --> 00:05:18.480
RECOVERY Trial (U.K. Approach). The budget was twenty million dollars.

50
00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:23.630
The timeline was six months. Forty-eight thousand patients were enrolled.

51
00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:28.960
Multiple treatments were found, including dexamethasone (saved over one million lives).

52
00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:38.140
The cost per patient was five hundred dollars. The N.I.H. spent one hundred thirty-three times more per patient and achieved

53
00:05:38.140 --> 00:05:46.220
infinitely less (dividing by zero trials completed produces infinity, which is fitting because that's how long patients

54
00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:55.980
waited). This efficiency gap is not unique to RECOVERY. A systematic review of sixty-four pragmatic trials found a median

55
00:05:55.980 --> 00:06:04.240
cost of ninety-seven dollars per patient. The British cured disease for the price of a nice dinner per patient.

56
00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:09.780
America spent the price of a luxury SUV per patient and cured nothing.

57
00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:16.330
The problem isn't that curing disease is hard. The N.I.H. doesn't have a capability problem.

58
00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:22.970
It has a shopping addiction. The Death Toll: Opportunity Cost of Misallocation.

59
00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:31.480
When you misallocate forty billion dollars a year into low-efficiency research instead of high-efficiency clinical trials,

60
00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:40.400
you aren't just wasting money. You are composting people. Cost Per quality-adjusted life year, or Q.A.L.Y.

61
00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:45.320
The standard N.I.H. portfolio costs fifty thousand dollars per Q.A.L.Y.

62
00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:51.120
(and that's being generous). Pragmatic platform trials cost four dollars per Q.A.L.Y.

63
00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:59.840
Every one dollar spent on efficient pragmatic trials buys twelve thousand five hundred times more health than a dollar spent

64
00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:08.780
on the current N.I.H. portfolio. The ADAPTABLE trial proved pragmatic trials cost thirty times less than traditional randomized

65
00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:15.400
controlled trials, or R.C.T.s (fourteen million dollars versus four hundred twenty million dollars).

66
00:07:16.420 --> 00:07:22.980
Same science. Fewer catered meetings. The "Death Equivalent" of Budget Misallocation.

67
00:07:23.560 --> 00:07:32.540
The annual opportunity cost is approximately one hundred million Q.A.L.Y.s and approximately seven million death-equivalents

68
00:07:32.980 --> 00:07:41.740
per year. Even the most conservative estimate is approximately ten million Q.A.L.Y.s and approximately seven hundred thousand

69
00:07:41.740 --> 00:07:49.960
preventable deaths annually. You are operating at approximately two percent of your potential capacity to save lives.

70
00:07:50.620 --> 00:07:57.960
If your car ran at two percent efficiency, you'd walk. If your heart ran at two percent efficiency, you'd die.

71
00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:04.660
Your medical research system runs at two percent efficiency, and you gave it a raise.

72
00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:12.720
The Translation Crisis: All Theory, No Medicine. The problem is not a shortage of ideas.

73
00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:19.620
It is a shortage of anyone bothering to test them. There are more than twenty thousand F.D.A.

74
00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:26.720
approved drugs known to be safe in humans. There are approximately four thousand seven hundred to six thousand eight hundred

75
00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:35.120
clinically-tested candidates for repurposable compounds (including three thousand four hundred twenty-two marketed drugs)

76
00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:39.360
gathering dust. There are hundreds of plausible dementia interventions.

77
00:08:40.460 --> 00:08:43.860
There are zero clinical trials available for your grandma in St.

78
00:08:43.940 --> 00:08:50.540
Louis. You have a warehouse of safe molecules that have never been systematically tested for new uses.

79
00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:56.190
The warehouse is full. The testing facility is closed for a team-building retreat.

80
00:08:56.720 --> 00:09:04.920
The N.I.H. is, by its own metrics, a publishing house that occasionally dabbles in medicine: Two point five million papers

81
00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:09.660
are published annually. Approximately fifty new treatments are approved annually.

82
00:09:10.520 --> 00:09:15.800
The conversion rate from paper to patient is zero point zero zero two percent.

83
00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:24.520
For every fifty thousand papers, one drug reaches a human. The rest become citations in other papers, which become citations

84
00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:28.900
in other papers, in an infinite loop of academics reading each other's homework.

85
00:09:29.980 --> 00:09:36.440
Plenty of Knowledge. No Translation. Approximately five percent of patients have participated in trials.

86
00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:41.080
Approximately fifty percent of patients are willing to participate if invited.

87
00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:46.000
Only nine to eleven percent of patients are actually invited.

88
00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:50.820
Half of all patients would join a clinical trial if asked. Almost nobody asks.

89
00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:56.950
It's like having a room full of volunteers and a clipboard, and choosing to study the clipboard.

90
00:09:57.660 --> 00:10:01.940
Understanding disease and curing disease are completely different activities.

91
00:10:02.740 --> 00:10:08.320
One requires microscopes. The other requires giving people medicine and seeing if they stop dying.

92
00:10:09.020 --> 00:10:14.660
The N.I.H. chose microscopes. The Public Goods vs. Club Goods Scam.

93
00:10:15.620 --> 00:10:19.660
The N.I.H. claims to produce "public goods" like open science and training.

94
00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:27.620
What it actually produces is a free R and D department for pharmaceutical companies, funded by the same people who then

95
00:10:27.620 --> 00:10:35.660
pay full price for the drugs. Taxpayers funded research behind ninety-nine percent of new drugs (three hundred fifty-six

96
00:10:35.660 --> 00:10:40.480
drugs, twenty-ten to twenty-nineteen). Industry patents the winners.

97
00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:47.800
The public pays again through high drug prices. When research leads to a dead end, the public eats the loss.

98
00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:54.400
You pay for the lottery ticket. They cash the winnings. The only things the N.I.H.

99
00:10:54.400 --> 00:11:02.280
SHOULD fund are things private companies CANNOT profitably fund: Open pragmatic trial platforms are thirty times cheaper

100
00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:06.320
than traditional trials, yet receive minimal N.I.H. funding.

101
00:11:06.580 --> 00:11:14.280
There are over four thousand seven hundred clinically-tested compounds for re-purposing generic drugs where no patent equals

102
00:11:14.280 --> 00:11:22.680
no profit equals no interest. Comparative effectiveness data from head-to-head trials reveal which drugs actually work best

103
00:11:23.500 --> 00:11:28.700
(pharma's nightmare). These are the ONLY things the N.I.H. should be funding.

104
00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:34.400
Instead, the N.I.H. explicitly avoids them to "not crowd out industry."

105
00:11:35.380 --> 00:11:42.120
Translation: "We won't run the trials that would lower drug prices, because the people who set drug prices asked us nicely.".

106
00:11:43.460 --> 00:11:47.750
The Patient Disconnect: Zero Correlation with Health Outcomes.

107
00:11:48.380 --> 00:11:55.380
The correlation between N.I.H. funding priorities and actual disease burden is zero point zero seven.

108
00:11:56.320 --> 00:12:03.600
That is not a typo. On a scale of zero (random) to one (perfect), the N.I.H.

109
00:12:03.880 --> 00:12:09.890
scores "basically a coin flip." A dartboard would allocate research funding more rationally.

110
00:12:10.760 --> 00:12:15.660
What patients want. Medicine that works. Access to experimental therapies.

111
00:12:16.200 --> 00:12:21.100
Trials they can join. A cure before they die. What N.I.H. funds.

112
00:12:21.980 --> 00:12:27.160
Molecular mechanisms. Papers in prestigious journals. Research empires.

113
00:12:27.580 --> 00:12:32.140
Institutional overhead. Patients don't control the money. Committees do.

114
00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:36.460
And committees optimize for the continued existence of committees.

115
00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:42.420
This is true of all committees, everywhere, since the invention of committees.

116
00:12:42.960 --> 00:12:46.580
Nobody has ever convened a committee that recommended fewer committees.

117
00:12:47.620 --> 00:12:55.980
The Track Record. Since 1970, the N.I.H. has spent over one point one trillion dollars (inflation-adjusted).

118
00:12:56.740 --> 00:13:05.180
Diseases eradicated: Zero. This isn't because eradicating disease is impossible: The W.H.O.

119
00:13:05.180 --> 00:13:13.440
eradicated smallpox for three hundred million dollars. Jonas Salk developed the polio vaccine in a university lab and gave

120
00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:18.000
it away for free (which is why nobody at the N.I.H. talks about him at parties).

121
00:13:18.640 --> 00:13:23.760
Veterinarians have eradicated multiple animal diseases while the N.I.H.

122
00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:30.380
was forming subcommittees about forming subcommittees. Cows have better healthcare outcomes than you do.

123
00:13:31.540 --> 00:13:36.420
What Would Actually Work. The U.K. RECOVERY trial already proved it.

124
00:13:37.240 --> 00:13:40.860
Five hundred dollars per patient instead of forty-one thousand dollars.

125
00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:45.460
One hundred days instead of years. Over one million lives saved.

126
00:13:46.300 --> 00:13:51.240
The blueprint exists. It is not theoretical. It happened. In England.

127
00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:55.500
During a pandemic. While Americans were arguing about horse dewormer.

128
00:13:56.320 --> 00:14:03.820
The recipe: Pay for results: Treatment equals payment. No treatment equals no payment.

129
00:14:04.070 --> 00:14:10.280
Radical, I know. Publish everything: Including failures (especially failures).

130
00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:14.830
Eliminate gatekeepers: Let patients vote with participation.

131
00:14:15.740 --> 00:14:22.360
Automate administration: Smart contracts instead of committees (committees cannot be trusted; see above).
